Technical Design: DLT Deduplication & Historical Backfill
1. Problem Statement
The current DLT Streaming Table (using APPEND FLOW) contains duplicates. Due to a 30-day source retention limit and multi-year target history, a standard DLT "Full Refresh" is impossible without causing massive data loss. Standard APPLY CHANGES logic is excluded due to performance overhead at high volumes.

2. Proposed Solution: The "Hybrid Seed" Refresh
We will perform a managed migration that "seeds" a clean target table with historical data while simultaneously attaching a filtered stream to capture new data.

3. Execution Steps
Phase	Action	Purpose
I: Preparation	Stop the DLT pipeline and create a deduplicated backup of the target table (target_backup).	Ensures a "Golden Record" exists and prevents concurrent writes.
II: Code Change	Implement two append_flow functions: one static (spark.read) for the backup and one streaming (dlt.read_stream) with a date filter.	Allows DLT to re-build the table metadata from scratch using both history and current stream.
III: Re-Base	Trigger a Full Refresh in the DLT UI.	Clears the corrupted table and populates it with deduplicated history + recent 30-day data.
IV: Finalization	Remove the backup flow and the date filter; execute a Standard Start.	Returns the pipeline to a clean, high-performance "append-only" state.

Export to Sheets

4. Implementation Code (Step III Phase)
Python

import dlt
from pyspark.sql.functions import col

# Flow A: Historical Backfill (Runs once during Full Refresh)
@dlt.append_flow(target="target_table")
def seed_history():
    return spark.read.table("main.schema.target_backup")

# Flow B: Incremental Stream (Captures live data post-backup)
@dlt.append_flow(target="target_table")
def filtered_stream():
    return (
        dlt.read_stream("source_bronze")
           .filter(col("source_timestamp") > "2026-01-01 00:00:00") # Backup Max TS
    )
5. Why this is the "Best" Option
Zero Data Loss: By seeding history via a static read during the refresh, we bypass the 30-day retention limit of the source.

High Performance: We avoid the state-store overhead and compute cost of APPLY CHANGES (SCD Type 1), maintaining the speed of a standard APPEND.

Checkpoint Integrity: A "Full Refresh" creates a clean checkpoint. By the time we remove the filter in Step IV, the stream is already past the "duplicate zone."

Metadata Safety: It follows the native DLT lifecycle, ensuring the table remains fully managed and compatible with Unity Catalog.

6. Critical Engineering Guardrails
⚠️ Warning: Step IV MUST be a Standard Start. If a Full Refresh is triggered after removing the backup code, the historical data (pre-30 days) will be deleted.

⚠️ Filter Precision: The timestamp filter in filtered_stream must align exactly with the MAX(timestamp) in the backup table to prevent gaps or double-counting at the cutover point.
